WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.733 --> 00:00:08.768
Back at the end of the bakery line, the packer has a small table where loaves wait to be boxed.

2
00:00:08.768 --> 00:00:09.594
It holds ten.

3
00:00:09.594 --> 00:00:17.089
As long as the oven waits for her slips, the table never overflows: she writes send me 10 more only when there's room.

4
00:00:17.600 --> 00:00:20.246
But not everything in a bakery reads slips.

5
00:00:20.246 --> 00:00:22.953
The thermometer in the oven is a chatterbox.

6
00:00:22.953 --> 00:00:27.815
It reports the temperature every millisecond, whether anyone is looking or not.

7
00:00:27.815 --> 00:00:32.368
Nobody has ever asked it to slow down, and it wouldn't listen if they did.

8
00:00:32.533 --> 00:00:35.107
Its readings go to a screen in the shop.

9
00:00:35.107 --> 00:00:40.383
The screen asks for one reading at a time, and takes 10 milliseconds to draw each.

10
00:00:40.383 --> 00:00:48.297
In Reactor, Flux.interval plays the thermometer: it sends a number, called a tick, every millisecond, whatever you request.

11
00:00:48.467 --> 00:00:50.754
Reading 0 is asked for, and drawn.

12
00:00:50.754 --> 00:00:58.421
Reading 1 arrives before anyone asked for it, and the stream fails: could not emit tick 1 due to lack of requests.

13
00:00:59.133 --> 00:01:04.211
Reactor has a family of operators for this, with backpressure right in their names.

14
00:01:04.211 --> 00:01:07.391
And here's the question that's easy to be shy about.

15
00:01:07.391 --> 00:01:10.939
Backpressure means the slow side slows down the fast side.

16
00:01:10.939 --> 00:01:14.243
So surely one of them makes the thermometer slow down?

17
00:01:14.933 --> 00:01:20.196
Let's try the one everyone reaches for first: onBackpressureBuffer, with no size.

18
00:01:20.196 --> 00:01:22.340
The screen takes twenty readings.

19
00:01:22.340 --> 00:01:23.444
What does it get?

20
00:01:26.467 --> 00:01:29.363
Readings 0, 1, 2, all the way to 19.

21
00:01:29.363 --> 00:01:31.213
In order, and no error.

22
00:01:31.213 --> 00:01:32.581
It looks perfect.

23
00:01:32.581 --> 00:01:34.109
That's the problem.

24
00:01:34.267 --> 00:01:39.242
When the screen drew reading 19, the thermometer was at reading 216.

25
00:01:39.242 --> 00:01:48.680
Nearly 200 readings were waiting in a queue with no limit, about 90 more every 100 milliseconds, for as long as the program runs.

26
00:01:49.467 --> 00:01:54.298
It's our doorstep with fourteen loaves, back again, with more every second.

27
00:01:54.298 --> 00:01:57.584
We fixed the smoke alarm by taking out the battery.

28
00:01:57.584 --> 00:01:58.743
The noise stopped.

29
00:01:58.743 --> 00:01:59.774
The fire didn't.

30
00:01:59.774 --> 00:02:02.093
So no error doesn't mean no problem.

31
00:02:02.093 --> 00:02:05.443
The pile just moved into memory, where nobody looks.

32
00:02:06.200 --> 00:02:08.338
So, back to the packer's table.

33
00:02:08.338 --> 00:02:15.030
When a belt won't stop, the table fills, and someone has to decide what happens to the next item.

34
00:02:15.030 --> 00:02:17.099
There are four honest answers.

35
00:02:17.099 --> 00:02:18.341
Keep a small pile.

36
00:02:18.341 --> 00:02:19.583
Throw it in a bin.

37
00:02:19.583 --> 00:02:21.031
Keep only the newest.

38
00:02:21.031 --> 00:02:22.204
Or stop the line.

39
00:02:22.400 --> 00:02:25.624
Each onBackpressure operator is one of those answers.

40
00:02:25.624 --> 00:02:29.881
It asks the thermometer for everything, so nothing overflows above it.

41
00:02:29.881 --> 00:02:34.687
Then it decides what happens to the readings nobody further down has asked for.

42
00:02:35.467 --> 00:02:39.224
The small pile is onBackpressureBuffer with a size, say 10.

43
00:02:39.224 --> 00:02:42.536
While the screen draws reading 0, the pile fills up.

44
00:02:42.536 --> 00:02:48.777
Then a reading finds no room, so the line stops: the thermometer is cut off, and an error is sent.

45
00:02:48.733 --> 00:02:51.535
Does the screen still get the readings on the pile?

46
00:02:54.600 --> 00:02:55.150
It does.

47
00:02:55.150 --> 00:02:57.969
The error waits its turn behind the pile.

48
00:02:57.969 --> 00:03:03.264
The screen got readings 0 to 11, in order, and then: the receiver is overrun.

49
00:03:03.264 --> 00:03:05.464
Nothing vanished without a word.

50
00:03:06.200 --> 00:03:08.374
The bin is onBackpressureDrop.

51
00:03:08.374 --> 00:03:13.446
A reading that arrives while the screen isn't asking goes straight in.

52
00:03:13.446 --> 00:03:18.012
The screen saw 0, 11, 22, 34, and 202 readings went in the bin.

53
00:03:18.012 --> 00:03:18.954
Why 11 apart?

54
00:03:18.954 --> 00:03:24.751
When the screen asks, the bin has nothing on hand, so it waits for the next one.

55
00:03:25.533 --> 00:03:28.563
Keep only the newest is onBackpressureLatest.

56
00:03:28.563 --> 00:03:32.873
It has one slot, and each new reading pushes out the one before.

57
00:03:32.873 --> 00:03:37.991
When the screen asks, the newest is ready: 0, 10, 20, 30, about every tenth.

58
00:03:38.733 --> 00:03:41.240
And stop the line is onBackpressureError.

59
00:03:41.240 --> 00:03:44.114
It fails at the first reading nobody asked for.

60
00:03:44.114 --> 00:03:46.988
That's for belts where falling behind is a bug.

61
00:03:48.467 --> 00:03:51.532
So, does any of them slow the thermometer down?

62
00:03:51.532 --> 00:03:52.053
Not one.

63
00:03:52.053 --> 00:03:57.271
Honest names would be: keep a pile, use the bin, keep the newest, stop the line.

64
00:03:57.271 --> 00:03:58.705
They're loss policies.

65
00:03:58.705 --> 00:04:01.705
They decide what to keep, and what to give up.

66
00:04:02.467 --> 00:04:04.821
And here the bakery picture breaks.

67
00:04:04.821 --> 00:04:07.915
A full table in a real bakery is hard to miss.

68
00:04:07.915 --> 00:04:16.524
A buffer with no size is a table nobody can see, and it grows until the heap, the memory where Java keeps its objects, runs out.

69
00:04:17.267 --> 00:04:26.367
Real backpressure needs a source that reads the slips, like the database cursor behind the nightly report, which fetches only the rows it's asked for.

70
00:04:26.367 --> 00:04:30.613
That's backpressure for free, so pick a source like that when you can.

71
00:04:31.400 --> 00:04:34.319
When you can't, ask what one missed item costs.

72
00:04:34.319 --> 00:04:37.549
A temperature is a value where newer replaces older.

73
00:04:37.549 --> 00:04:42.705
A buffer here would show the oven's temperature from a minute ago, very accurately.

74
00:04:42.705 --> 00:04:43.885
So keep the newest.

75
00:04:44.067 --> 00:04:50.277
A sample, like one visitor count out of hundreds, can go in the bin, as long as you count the drops.

76
00:04:50.277 --> 00:04:53.258
But an order from the website must never vanish.

77
00:04:53.258 --> 00:05:00.151
Give it a small pile that fails loudly, and a way to ask the website for everything after the last saved order.

78
00:05:00.933 --> 00:05:02.885
Here it is in Java and Kotlin.

79
00:05:02.885 --> 00:05:05.227
The screen keeps the newest reading.

80
00:05:05.227 --> 00:05:10.105
The web orders get a pile of 1,000, and the order that overflows is logged.

81
00:05:10.867 --> 00:05:13.816
So, does onBackpressure slow the source down?

82
00:05:13.816 --> 00:05:14.012
No.

83
00:05:14.012 --> 00:05:17.027
Only a source that reads the slips slows down.

84
00:05:17.027 --> 00:05:24.760
For one that can't, you choose what to lose when the table is full: a small pile, a bin, the newest, or stop the line.

85
00:05:24.933 --> 00:05:25.747
Quick check.

86
00:05:25.747 --> 00:05:29.002
A screen in the shop shows the oven temperature.

87
00:05:29.002 --> 00:05:34.833
The thermometer sends 200 readings a second, and the screen redraws 10 times a second.

88
00:05:34.833 --> 00:05:36.053
Which policy fits?

89
00:05:39.067 --> 00:05:41.421
Keep the newest: onBackpressureLatest.

90
00:05:41.421 --> 00:05:47.059
At every redraw, the screen wants the temperature now, not the next old reading in a queue.
